  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY...\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite imagery and upper air \nanalysis clearly show a large and strong mid/upper ridge across the \nsouthern/central Rockies and into the southern/central plains. 500 \nmb heights approaching 600 dam is one of a number of factors showing \nhow supportive this current pattern is for hot conditions across the \nforecast area. As a result of recent rainfall, ample \nevapotranspiration will keep dewpoints well into the 70s across a \nlarge portion of the area while afternoon temperatures easily rise \ninto the upper 90s to low 100s. As a result of this dewpoint and air \ntemperature combo, heat indices this afternoon will likely be over \n105 across the entire forecast area. Across southern and southeast \nKansas where higher dewpoints are expected, heat indices this \nafternoon should rise to at least 110. Despite widespread subsidence \nunderneath the aforementioned ridge, the environment this afternoon \nwill be uncapped with surface temps nearing convective temps, \nprimarily across southern Kansas into northern Oklahoma. As a \nresult, PoPs will be around 10% for the potential of a brief, \nisolated shower or storm during peak diurnal heating this afternoon \nand early evening mainly along and south of US-54.\n\nThe pattern is not expected to change much Sunday through Tuesday \nwith a strong upper anti-cyclone and very warm mid-level \ntemperatures supporting hot surface air temperatures each afternoon \nand early evening. With slightly less moisture Sunday through \nTuesday, heat indices will likely remain below 110 despite highs \neach afternoon rising above 100 degrees across the majority of the \nforecast area. The hottest day appears to be Monday as guidance \nsuggests the mid/upper ridge will be centered over Kansas and \nOklahoma. Record highs on Monday are unlikely to be broken, but it's \npossible few locations could see their first temperatures above 105 \nsince August of 2023. In addition to hot daytime temperatures, \nnighttime temperatures are anticipated to be near record highs with \nmany locations each night only dropping into the upper 70s to low \n80s. \n\n     TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY..\n\nThe strong mid/upper ridge will begin to break down on Tuesday \nlimiting the hottest conditions to southern Kansas for Tuesday \nafternoon. Additionally, the breakdown of this ridge will open the \ndoor for low storm chances to return as soon as Tuesday \nevening/night. The primary ridge axis will continue to retrograde \ntowards the Four Corners region during the latter half of next week \nallowing for a continuation of periodic low storm chances each \nevening and night Wednesday through the weekend. Despite the ridge \nshifting westward, temperatures will generally remain just a tad \nabove average with afternoon highs in the upper 90s and overnight \nlows in the mid to upper 70s. It's too early to determine the \ncoverage and intensity of these potential storms as that will likely \nbe determined by mesoscale features that will more apparent as we \napproach this time frame, so stay tuned for updates to the forecast \nover the coming days.",
